Optimise and tape-test the :00 strategy: liquidity fine, edge decays to zero

Two follow-ups: optimise the strategy, and test it against the real Aug 4-5
tape rather than displayed quotes.

1. THE HEADLINE +7.81c WAS A FULL-SAMPLE NUMBER

Splitting it properly, with the grid searched on TRAIN ONLY and valid/test
never consulted:

  train  n=175  win 0.8629   +9.56c  P(<=0) 0.0001
  valid  n= 92  win 0.8261   +5.70c  P(<=0) 0.0956
  test   n= 93  win 0.7634   -0.15c  P(<=0) 0.5229
  HELD OUT       n=185       +2.76c  95% CI [-2.73, +8.33]  P(<=0) 0.1718

On data never used to build or choose anything the edge is +2.76c and NOT
significant; in the test period alone it is zero. The monotone decay flagged
in the previous commit is confirmed and is now the dominant fact about this
strategy. The full-sample figure remains correct but must not be read as an
expectation.

2. OPTIMISATION ADDS NOTHING

A 243-configuration grid over entry band, wait and ask band, searched on train
only and frozen, gained -0.10c per contract out of sample against the plain
version (+2.76c vs +2.86c). The search fitted train noise. Its train winner
does pass a best-of-grid permutation (P=0.0000 against a permuted best of
+0.49c) - which shows the TRAIN effect is real, not that it generalises.

3. LIQUIDITY IS NOT THE CONSTRAINT

Tested against trades_lsm.parquet, 2,114,639 real prints:

  7 signals with tape coverage, 7/7 had prints, 6/7 had depth at our price
  median depth 770 contracts, 71.4% had >= 15
  fill ratio at q15 0.7771, backtest +$5.56 -> realistic +$4.35
  q5 0.857 fill / q15 0.777 / q30 0.746

Caveats stated in the report: the VWAP measure averages in prints better than
our limit so it flatters execution, and n=7 - the tape only covers markets LSM
actually traded. It supports a liquidity conclusion, not a P&L one.

4. A TIMESTAMP BUG CAUGHT BEFORE PUBLICATION

The first tape run returned zero prints in every window. trades_lsm.ts is
epoch SECONDS, not milliseconds: the compaction did .astype("int64") // 10**6
assuming datetime64[ns] while this pandas stores datetime64[us], so the
division had already produced seconds. DATA.md documented it wrongly and is
corrected.

Same unit-confusion class that invalidated PTC v1. Caught because `ml` came
out as 29,734,390 minutes - about 56 years - instead of a plausible number.

REVISED STATUS
  liquidity supports q15                     holds
  momentum / volume / spread filters         refuted, all inert
  the 2-minute wait is load-bearing          refuted, P=0.1664 vs 1 minute
  grid optimisation improves it              refuted, -0.10c out of sample
  the :00 effect is exploitable              UNRESOLVED, +2.76c held out

139 tests pass.

